  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 111 PM CDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nA cold front was bisecting northwest Kansas this morning while a \ntrough of low pressure extended SSW of this frontal boundary. As the \nlow level flow backs late this afternoon/early this evening we may \nsee some pooling of low level moisture allowing the cap to weaken \nsufficiently. Scattered thunderstorms are expected to develop \nalong the frontal zone after 4 pm. Meanwhile, another hot day \nis anticipated south of the front where a deeply mixed pbl is \nexpected once again. This deeply mixed pbl will become \nmoderately unstable with large DCAPE. Strong downdrafts are \nlikely with any storm that develops while the threat for large \nhail appears slightly less favorable given modest mid-lvl lapse \nrates. PWATs around 2 inches or above the 97th percentile will \nsupport efficient rainfall with some localized flooding concerns\nwhere training occurs. Another cluster of storms is expected to\ndevelop across the Central High Plains this evening as the \nmid/upper trough moves into the Northern Rockies. This cluster \nmay be aided by a developing LLJ nosing into central KS allowing\nthe storms to persist arriving across the area after midnight. \nThe frontal boundary is progged to become stationary across the \nFlint Hills late tonight. As mid-lvl lapse rates steepen through\nthe night, we could still see a few lingering strong/severe \nstorms across the area.\n\nSome showers and storms may be ongoing early in the day on Sunday \nwith considerable convective debris leading to little or reduced \ninsolation and temperatures were trended lower. The front will \nbecome a focus for showers and storms once again as we move through \nthe day. An earlier onset appears likely which may limit the threat \nfor more widespread severe weather before the focus shifts south and \neast of the area late in the day. Maintained some low pops into \nSunday evening with some lingering mid-lvl waa as the pv anomaly \nmoves across the area. Much cooler air will arrive in the wake of \nthe cold front while a slower fropa will result in another mild day \nin parts of southern KS on Sunday.\n\nMonday-Wednesday...a stable post-frontal regime is anticipated as we \nmove through the middle of the work-week with cool weather expected. \nAfternoon highs are expected to top out in the 70s with lows in the \n50s.\n\nThursday-Sunday...The mid/upper ridge is progged to reside over the \nSouthern Plains while nosing into the Central Plains as we move into \nthe latter portion of the week. This will allow temperatures to \nbegin to moderate with seasonably mild values anticipated. Flow \naround this ridge may help steer some shortwave troughs toward the \narea late in the week and into the weekend and some modest pops were \nmaintained through the period.",
